=== lesson
id: py-errors
title: Reading error messages
summary: Every programmer sees errors all the time. Learn what an error is, how to read Python's error report from the bottom up, and a step-by-step way to fix the five errors beginners meet most.

=== learn
### An error means Python got stuck
Sometimes Python reaches a line that it can't carry out. Then it stops the program and prints an **error message**: a report that says what went wrong and where.

Here is the mistake from the last lesson, text without quotes:
```python
print(Hi)
```
```output
Traceback (most recent call last):
  File "main.py", line 1, in <module>
    print(Hi)
          ^^
NameError: name 'Hi' is not defined
```
Python's word for an error like this is an **exception**. When a line causes one, we say the line **raises** an error. In this course, *error* and *exception* mean the same thing.

Errors are normal. Every programmer sees them many times a day, and they can't harm your computer. An error message is Python telling you where it got stuck. This lesson teaches you to read it.

### A program stops at the first error
Python runs a program from top to bottom. When a line raises an error, Python stops right there:
- Lines **before** the error have already run. Their output stays on the screen.
- The line with the error, and every line **after** it, never run.

```python
print("Line 1")
print("Line 2")
prnt("Line 3")
print("Line 4")
```
```output
Line 1
Line 2
Traceback (most recent call last):
  File "main.py", line 3, in <module>
    prnt("Line 3")
    ^^^^
NameError: name 'prnt' is not defined. Did you mean: 'print'?
```
Lines 1 and 2 printed. Line 3 has a typo, `prnt` instead of `print`, so Python stopped there. `Line 4` never appeared, even though line 4 itself is fine.

> Fixing line 3 is all it takes to make line 4 run. Nothing is wrong with line 4: Python never reached it.

### Reading a traceback from the bottom up
The full error report is called a **traceback**. Here is the one from the last step again:
```output
Traceback (most recent call last):
  File "main.py", line 3, in <module>
    prnt("Line 3")
    ^^^^
NameError: name 'prnt' is not defined. Did you mean: 'print'?
```
Read it **from the bottom up**:
1. **The last line says what went wrong.** Before the colon is the **error type**, `NameError`, which names the kind of problem. After the colon is the **message**, which gives the details. Python sometimes adds a guess, like `Did you mean: 'print'?`.
2. **Above it is a copy of the failing line**, with **markers** (`^^^^`) under the exact part that caused the error.
3. **Above that is the line number**, `line 3`. Lines are counted from 1, starting at the top.

You can skim the rest: `File "main.py"` names the file the code is in, `in <module>` means "in the main part of the program", and the first line is a heading.

> Marker lines are a newer Python feature (Python 3.11 and later), and not every error gets them. The last line and the line number are always there, so read those first.

### SyntaxError: Python can't read the code
**Syntax** is the set of rules for how code must be written: brackets come in pairs, quotes come in pairs, and values are separated by commas. A **SyntaxError** means a line breaks those rules, so Python can't understand it.

Python checks the **whole file** before it runs any of it. If it finds a SyntaxError, **nothing runs**, not even the lines above the mistake:
```python
print("Start")
print("Hello"
print("End")
```
```output
  File "main.py", line 2
    print("Hello"
         ^
SyntaxError: '(' was never closed
```
`Start` was never printed. There is no `Traceback` heading either, because the program never started. The `^` points at the bracket that was opened and never closed.

Because Python reads the whole file first, a SyntaxError is always the **first** error you see, even when other mistakes come earlier in the file. Those other errors only show up once the program can run.

### Common SyntaxError messages
The message after `SyntaxError:` tells you which rule was broken:

| Mistake | Code | Message |
| --- | --- | --- |
| missing closing bracket | `print("Hi"` | `'(' was never closed` |
| extra closing bracket | `print("Hi"))` | `unmatched ')'` |
| missing closing quote | `print("Hi)` | `unterminated string literal (detected at line 1)` |
| quotes that don't match | `print('Hi")` | `unterminated string literal (detected at line 1)` |
| missing comma between values | `print("Age:" 12)` | `invalid syntax. Perhaps you forgot a comma?` |
| an apostrophe inside single quotes | `print('It's me')` | `unterminated string literal (detected at line 1)` |

A **string literal** is a string typed into your code between quotes. *Unterminated* means it never ends: Python can't find its closing quote. In the fourth row, the string starts with `'`, so only another `'` can end it. In the last row, the apostrophe in *It's* ends the string early, so the final `'` starts a new string that never ends. Text with an apostrophe belongs in double quotes: `print("It's me")`.

Later lessons show lines that must end with a colon `:`. Forgetting that colon is one more SyntaxError: `expected ':'`.

### IndentationError: spaces at the start of a line
**Indentation** means spaces at the start of a line. Later lessons use indentation on purpose, to group lines together. Until then, every line must start at the very left edge.

A line that starts with spaces for no reason raises an **IndentationError**:
```python
print("Roses are red")
  print("Violets are blue")
```
```output
  File "main.py", line 2
    print("Violets are blue")
IndentationError: unexpected indent
```
*Unexpected indent* means "this line is pushed in, and I didn't expect that". Fix it by deleting the spaces at the start of line 2.

Two details:
- The report shows the line *without* its extra spaces, so look at your own code to see them.
- An IndentationError is a kind of SyntaxError. It is found **before anything runs**, so `Roses are red` was never printed.

### NameError: Python doesn't know that name
A word without quotes, like `print`, is a **name**. When Python meets a name, it looks for something it already knows by that name. If there isn't one, it raises a **NameError**. *Not defined* means Python has never been told what that name means.

Three mistakes cause most NameErrors:

| Code | Mistake | Last line of the report |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `prnt("Hi")` | a typo | `NameError: name 'prnt' is not defined. Did you mean: 'print'?` |
| `Print("Hi")` | a wrong capital letter | `NameError: name 'Print' is not defined. Did you mean: 'print'?` |
| `print(Hi)` | text without quotes | `NameError: name 'Hi' is not defined` |

The message shows the unknown name in quotes, so you know exactly which word to check. In the last row, `Hi` was meant to be text, so the fix is to add quotes: `print("Hi")`.

### TypeError: mixing text and numbers with +
`+` does two different jobs. Between two numbers it adds: `print(5 + 5)` shows `10`. Between two strings it **joins** them, which is called **concatenation**: `print("tea" + "cup")` shows `teacup`, with no space added.

Between a string and a number, Python won't guess which job you meant:
```python
print("5" + 5)
```
```output
Traceback (most recent call last):
  File "main.py", line 1, in <module>
    print("5" + 5)
          ~~~~^~~
TypeError: can only concatenate str (not "int") to str
```
`str` is Python's short name for a string. `int`, short for **integer**, means a whole number. So the message says: a string can only be joined to another string, not to an int. In the marker line, `^` points at the `+` and `~` marks the values on each side.

To fix it, use a comma, `print("Age:", 12)`, or make both sides strings, `print("Age: " + "12")`.

> With the number first, `5 + "5"`, the message is `unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'int' and 'str'`. An **operand** is a value on either side of a sign like `+`. Same problem, same fixes.

### ZeroDivisionError: dividing by zero
In Python, `/` means divide: `print(10 / 2)` shows `5.0`. (Division always gives a number with a decimal point. The Types lesson explains why.)

Dividing by zero has no answer, so Python raises a **ZeroDivisionError**:
```python
print("Start")
print("Each:", 10 / 0)
print("End")
```
```output
Start
Traceback (most recent call last):
  File "main.py", line 2, in <module>
    print("Each:", 10 / 0)
                   ~~~^~~
ZeroDivisionError: division by zero
```
Look closely: `Each:` was **not** printed. Before print shows anything, it works out every value inside its brackets. It failed while working out `10 / 0`, so this print showed nothing at all. As always, the line after the error (`print("End")`) never ran.

### Fixing errors step by step
Use the same method for every error:
1. **Read the last line.** The error type tells you the kind of problem. The message gives the details.
2. **Go to the line number** in your code.
3. **Look where the markers point**, if there are any.
4. **Check the usual causes** for that error type (see the table).
5. **Change one thing, then run the program again.**
6. **A new error means progress.** Python stops at the first error, so a later mistake only shows up once the earlier one is fixed. Repeat until the program runs.

| Error type | Usual causes |
| --- | --- |
| SyntaxError | a missing or extra bracket, a missing quote, a missing comma |
| IndentationError | spaces at the start of a line |
| NameError | a typo, a wrong capital letter, text without quotes |
| TypeError | a string and a number joined with `+` |
| ZeroDivisionError | dividing by 0 |

=== keypoints
- An error (an **exception**) stops the program. Output from the lines before it stays; the failing line and every line after it never run.
- Read a **traceback** from the bottom up: the last line gives the error type and the message, the lines above give a copy of the failing line and its line number.
- **SyntaxError** and **IndentationError**: Python can't read the code, so *nothing* runs, not even the lines above the mistake.
- **NameError**: Python doesn't know a name. Look for a typo, a wrong capital letter, or text without quotes.
- **TypeError** from `"5" + 5`: `+` can't join a string and a number. Use a comma, or make both sides strings.
- **ZeroDivisionError**: dividing by 0. If the error happens inside a print(), that print shows nothing.
- To fix an error: read the last line, go to the line number, change one thing, run again.

=== example: An error halfway through
intro: Three lines print, then line 4 raises an error. Tap a note to highlight its line.
```python
print("Corner Cafe")
print("Coffee:", 3)
print("Cake:", 4)
print("Total: " + 7)
print("Thank you!")
```
1-3: These lines are fine. They run one after another, and their output stays on the screen when the error comes later.
4: `"Total: "` is a string and `7` is a number (an int). `+` can't join them, so Python raises a TypeError. The markers put `^` under the `+`. A fix: `print("Total:", 7)`.
5: Never runs. Python already stopped on line 4, so `Thank you!` is missing from the output.

=== example: A SyntaxError: nothing runs at all
intro: One missing bracket on line 3, and not even line 1 runs. Compare the output with the first example.
```python
print("Corner Cafe")
print("Coffee:", 3)
print("Cake:", 4
print("Thank you!")
```
1-2: Both lines are correct, but they never run. Python checks the whole file before running any of it, and the mistake on line 3 stops it before it starts.
3: The closing bracket is missing. The report says `'(' was never closed`, and the `^` points at the opening bracket that has no partner. The fix is to add `)` at the end of the line.
4: Doesn't run either. Notice that the report has no `Traceback` heading, because the program never started.

=== animation: Python stops at the first error
view: output
intro: Watch the output box. The highlighted line is the one about to run.
```python
print("Ready")
print("Set")
prnt("Go!")
print("Finished")
```
1: Python starts at the top. Line 1 is fine, so it will print Ready.
2: Ready is in the output. Line 2 will print Set.
3: Both lines are in the output, and they stay there. Now Python meets the name prnt. Nothing has that name (it's a typo for print), so this line will raise a NameError.

=== animation: An error inside print() prints nothing
view: output
intro: Before print shows anything, it works out every value inside its brackets. Watch line 4.
```python
print("Bill:", 60)
print("People:", 3)
print("Each pays:", 60 / 3)
print("Tip each:", 6 / 0)
print("Bye!")
```
1: Two values: the text Bill: and the number 60. print shows them with a space between.
2: Same again: People: 3.
3: First Python works out 60 / 3, which is 20.0. Only then does print show the line: Each pays: 20.0
4: Again Python works out the values first. "Tip each:" is ready, but 6 / 0 has no answer. The ZeroDivisionError happens before print can show anything, so not even Tip each: will appear.

=== task: mcq easy
title: Find the error type
prompt: A program stopped with this traceback. Which part names the **kind** of error?
```output
Traceback (most recent call last):
  File "main.py", line 2, in <module>
    Print("Bye")
    ^^^^^
NameError: name 'Print' is not defined. Did you mean: 'print'?
```
- `Traceback (most recent call last):` :: This is only the heading at the top. A NameError, a TypeError and a ZeroDivisionError all start with this same heading, so it can't tell you which kind of error this is.
- `line 2` :: The line number tells you *where* the error happened, not what kind of error it is.
- `^^^^^` :: The markers point at the part of the line that caused the error. They show where, not what kind.
* `NameError` :: Right. The error type is at the start of the last line, before the colon. NameError means Python met a name it doesn't know.
hint: Read the traceback from the bottom up.
hint: The last line has two parts, split by a colon: the kind of error, then the details.
right: The error type is always at the start of the last line, before the colon. Here it is `NameError`: Python doesn't know the name `Print`, because Python's function is `print` with a small p.
wrong: Start at the bottom. The last line is `NameError: name 'Print' is not defined...`. The word before the colon, `NameError`, is the error type. The text after the colon is the message with the details.

=== task: mcq easy
title: A word without quotes
prompt: What happens when Python runs this line?
code:
```python
print(Goodbye)
```
- It prints `Goodbye` :: Only text inside quotes is printed as written. Without quotes, Python reads Goodbye as the *name* of something and tries to look it up.
- `SyntaxError: invalid syntax` :: The line follows the writing rules: a name inside print's brackets is allowed, and the brackets are paired. Python can read it. The problem only shows up when the line runs.
* `NameError: name 'Goodbye' is not defined` :: Right. Without quotes, Goodbye is a name. Python looks for something called Goodbye, finds nothing, and stops.
- `TypeError: can only concatenate str (not "int") to str` :: A TypeError like this comes from joining a string and a number with `+`. This line has no `+` and no number.
hint: Quotes are what make text. What does Python do with a word that has no quotes?
hint: A word without quotes is a name. What happens when Python can't find something with that name?
right: Without quotes, `Goodbye` is treated as a name, not as text. Python looks for something called Goodbye, nothing has that name, so the line raises a NameError. Adding quotes, `print("Goodbye")`, fixes it.
wrong: Quotes are what make text. Without them, Python treats `Goodbye` as the name of something and tries to look it up. Nothing has that name, so the result is `NameError: name 'Goodbye' is not defined`.

=== task: predict easy
title: Text in quotes can't go wrong
code:
```python
print("10 / 0")
print("prnt")
print("Hi" + "!")
```
hint: Does Python calculate, or look up names, inside quotes?
hint: Lines 1 and 2 are text, printed exactly as written. On line 3, `+` joins two strings and adds no space.
right: Inside quotes everything is plain text: `10 / 0` is never divided and `prnt` is never looked up as a name, so neither line raises an error. Line 3 joins two strings with `+`, giving `Hi!` with no space between.
wrong: All three lines are safe. Quotes make text, and Python prints text exactly as written, so `10 / 0` and `prnt` come out unchanged. Line 3 joins the strings `"Hi"` and `"!"` into one: `Hi!`.
wrong[ZeroDivisionError: division by zero]: That error only happens when Python actually divides. Here `10 / 0` is inside quotes, so it's text, and Python prints it as written. None of the three lines raises an error.
wrong[ZeroDivisionError]: That error only happens when Python actually divides. Here `10 / 0` is inside quotes, so it's text, and Python prints it as written. None of the three lines raises an error.
wrong[10 / 0\nprnt\nHi !]: Close. `+` joins strings exactly as they are and adds no space (a comma in print is what adds a space). Line 3 prints `Hi!`.
wrong[10 / 0\nprint\nHi!]: Python never fixes typos inside a string. `"prnt"` is text, so it is printed exactly as written: `prnt`.

=== task: mcq easy
title: Which line is the problem on?
prompt: A program stopped with this report. On which line of the program is the problem?
```output
Apples
Pears
Traceback (most recent call last):
  File "main.py", line 3, in <module>
    print("Total: " + 10)
          ~~~~~~~~~~^~~~
TypeError: can only concatenate str (not "int") to str
```
- Line 1, because Python reports the first line of the program :: The line number in the report is where the error happened, not where the program starts.
- Line 2 :: Count again: the report says `line 3`. Lines 1 and 2 ran fine, which is why `Apples` and `Pears` were printed.
* Line 3 :: Right. The line under `Traceback` says `line 3`, and the copy of the failing line just below it shows exactly which code that is.
- The last line of the program, because errors are always at the end :: An error can be on any line. Python names the exact one, and here it's line 3.
hint: Look for the word `line` in the report.
hint: It's in the line that starts with `File "main.py"`.
right: The line starting with `File "main.py"` gives the line number: `line 3`. Lines are counted from 1 at the top. The copy of the failing line under it, `print("Total: " + 10)`, confirms which code it is.
wrong: Find the word `line` in the report: `File "main.py", line 3`. That's where the error happened. The copy of the failing line just below it shows the code itself.

=== task: multi easy
title: Spot the NameErrors
prompt: Choose **every** line that raises a **NameError** when it runs.
* `prnt("Hi")` :: Right. `prnt` is a typo. Python knows no name `prnt`, so it raises a NameError (and suggests `print`).
* `PRINT("Hi")` :: Right. Python is case-sensitive, so `PRINT` is a different name from `print`, and nothing has that name.
* `print(Hi)` :: Right. Without quotes, `Hi` is a name, not text, and nothing is called Hi.
- `print("Hi")` :: This line is correct: the right name, a pair of brackets, and text in quotes. It prints `Hi`.
- `print("prnt")` :: The typo is inside quotes, so it's only text. Python prints `prnt` and never looks it up as a name.
hint: There is more than one correct answer.
hint: A NameError happens when Python meets a word without quotes that it doesn't know.
hint: Words inside quotes are text, so Python never looks them up.
right: A NameError comes from a word *outside* quotes that Python doesn't know: a typo, the wrong capital letters, or text that lost its quotes. Inside quotes, every word is just text.
wrong: For each line, look at the words that are *not* in quotes. If one of them isn't exactly `print`, Python can't find it and raises a NameError. Words inside quotes are text and are never looked up.

=== task: fill easy
title: Close the bracket
prompt: This line stopped with `SyntaxError: '(' was never closed`. Fill the blank to fix it, so that the program prints:
```output
Hello
```
code:
```python
print("Hello"??
```
answer: )
wrong["]: The string already has both of its quotes: `"Hello"`. What's missing is the bracket that closes print.
hint: The message says a bracket was opened but never closed. Which bracket?
hint: Every `(` needs a `)` to match it.
hint: Type the one character that closes print's bracket.
right: `'(' was never closed` means Python found an opening bracket with no partner. Adding `)` closes print's bracket, so Python can read the line and run it.
wrong: The message `'(' was never closed` tells you exactly what's missing: the `)` that matches print's opening bracket. Type `)` in the blank.

=== task: code easy
title: Fix the missing quote
prompt: This program should print exactly:
```output
Good morning
Have a nice day!
```
But it stops with `SyntaxError: unterminated string literal (detected at line 1)`. Fix it.
tests: stdout
starter:
```python
print("Good morning)
print("Have a nice day!")
```
solution:
```python
print("Good morning")
print("Have a nice day!")
```
error[SyntaxError]: *Unterminated* means a string never ends: one of its quotes is missing. Look at line 1: where should the text end?
hint: Read the message: an *unterminated string literal* is a string with no closing quote.
hint: The report says line 1. Count the quotes on that line.
hint: Put a `"` straight after `morning`, before the bracket.
right: A string needs a quote at each end. Line 1 had only the opening `"`, so Python couldn't find where the text ended. With the closing quote added, Python can read the file and both lines run.
wrong: Line 1 is missing its closing quote: `print("Good morning")`. The quote goes after the last letter of the text and before the closing bracket.

=== task: predict easy
title: Errors hidden in comments
code:
```python
print("A")
# print(1 / 0)
print("B")
# prnt("C")
```
hint: Which lines does Python skip completely?
hint: A `#` turns the rest of the line into a comment, even when the code after it would cause an error.
hint: Only lines 1 and 3 run.
right: Lines 2 and 4 are comments, so Python never runs them: the division by zero and the typo can't cause errors there. Only `A` and `B` are printed.
wrong: A line that starts with `#` is a comment, and comments never run. So `1 / 0` is never divided and `prnt` is never looked up. Only lines 1 and 3 run.
wrong[A]: Line 2 never runs, because it's a comment, so it can't stop the program. Line 3 runs and prints `B`.
wrong[A\nB\nC]: Line 4 is a comment too, so `C` is never printed.
wrong[A\n1 / 0\nB]: Comments aren't printed. Line 2 is skipped completely.

=== task: cells easy
title: What prints before the error?
prompt: This program stops with an error. Type each line it prints **before** the traceback.
code:
```python
print("one")
print("two")
print(three)
print("four")
```
labels: output line 1 | output line 2
answer: one | two
wrong[one | four]: Python stops at the first error and never comes back. Line 3 raises a NameError, so line 4 never runs.
wrong[one | three]: Line 3 has no quotes around `three`, so Python treats it as a name. Nothing is called three, so line 3 raises a NameError and prints nothing. The two lines that do print are lines 1 and 2.
hint: Which line has a word without quotes?
hint: Python runs lines 1 and 2, then stops at line 3. Nothing after it runs.
hint: The output before the traceback is the output of lines 1 and 2.
right: Lines 1 and 2 run and print. Line 3 uses `three` without quotes, so it raises a NameError, and Python stops. The output that already appeared stays, but `four` is never printed.
wrong: Go line by line. Lines 1 and 2 are fine, so `one` and `two` are printed. Line 3 raises a NameError (no quotes around three), and Python stops there, so line 4 never runs.

=== task: parsons easy
title: Choose the lines that work
prompt: Build a program that prints exactly:
```output
Hello
Bye
```
Tap a line to add it to your program. Some lines contain errors, so leave those out.
lines:
```python
print("Hello")
print("Bye")
```
distractors:
```python
Print("Hello")
print("Bye"
```
hint: Check each line for the three rules from lesson 1: a lowercase print, a pair of brackets, and text in quotes.
hint: One distractor raises a NameError and the other a SyntaxError.
hint: `Print` has a capital P, and `print("Bye"` is missing its closing bracket.
right: The two correct lines use the lowercase name `print`, a pair of brackets, and text in quotes. `Print("Hello")` would raise a NameError, and `print("Bye"` a SyntaxError.
wrong: Leave out the lines with errors. `Print("Hello")` has a capital P, so it's a NameError. `print("Bye"` is missing its `)`, so it's a SyntaxError. Then put the correct lines in output order.

=== task: multi medium
title: Which lines can't Python even read?
prompt: Choose **every** line that causes a **SyntaxError**. (Remember: a SyntaxError means Python can't read the line, so nothing runs.)
* `print("Hello"` :: Right. The closing bracket is missing: `'(' was never closed`.
* `print('Hello")` :: Right. The string starts with `'`, so only another `'` can end it. Python never finds one: `unterminated string literal`.
- `print(Hello)` :: Python can read this line: a name inside paired brackets follows the rules. It fails only when it runs, with a NameError, because nothing is called Hello.
* `print("Total:" 10)` :: Right. Two values need a comma between them: `invalid syntax. Perhaps you forgot a comma?`
- `print(10 / 0)` :: The writing rules are all kept: a number, `/`, a number, inside paired brackets. It fails only when Python tries to divide, so it's a ZeroDivisionError.
hint: A SyntaxError is about how a line is *written*: brackets in pairs, quotes in pairs, commas between values.
hint: A line can be written correctly and still fail when it runs. Those errors have other types.
hint: Three lines break a writing rule. Two are written correctly but ask for something impossible.
right: A SyntaxError is about how the line is written: a missing bracket, quotes that don't match, a missing comma. `print(Hello)` and `print(10 / 0)` are written correctly, so Python can read them. They fail only while running, with a NameError and a ZeroDivisionError.
wrong: For each line, ask: does it break a writing rule? Brackets and quotes must come in matching pairs, and values need commas between them. A line that is written correctly but asks for something impossible, like an unknown name or dividing by zero, fails later with a different error type.

=== task: cells medium
title: Name that error
prompt: Pretend each line below is a whole program on its own. Which error does each one raise? Type one letter in each box:
- **S** for SyntaxError
- **I** for IndentationError
- **N** for NameError
- **T** for TypeError
- **Z** for ZeroDivisionError

Each letter is used once.
code:
```python
print("Score: " + 10)
print(Score)
print("Score:", 10 / 0)
  print("Score")
print("Score"))
```
labels: line 1 | line 2 | line 3 | line 4 | line 5
answer: T | N | Z | I | S
wrong[S | N | Z | I | T]: Lines 1 and 5 are swapped. Line 1 is written correctly (a string, `+`, a number, paired brackets), so Python can read it. It fails only when it runs, because `+` can't join a string and a number: a TypeError. Line 5 has an extra `)`, which breaks a writing rule: `SyntaxError: unmatched ')'`.
wrong[T | S | Z | I | S]: Line 2 follows the writing rules: a name inside paired brackets is allowed. It fails when it runs, because nothing is called Score (no quotes, so it's a name, not text). That's a NameError.
wrong[T | N | Z | S | I]: Lines 4 and 5 are swapped. Spaces at the start of a line give an IndentationError (`unexpected indent`). The extra `)` on line 5 is a SyntaxError: `unmatched ')'`.
hint: First find the two lines that break a writing rule. Python can't read those, so they raise SyntaxError or IndentationError.
hint: Of the other three: which one uses a name without quotes, which one joins text and a number with `+`, and which one divides by 0?
hint: Line 4 starts with spaces, and line 5 has one bracket too many. Line 1 mixes a string and a number with `+`.
right: Lines 4 and 5 break writing rules, so Python can't even read them: spaces at the start give an IndentationError, and an extra `)` gives a SyntaxError. The other three are written correctly and fail while running: `+` between a string and a number (TypeError), a name without quotes (NameError), and dividing by 0 (ZeroDivisionError).
wrong: Sort the lines into two groups first. Lines that break a writing rule can't be read: an extra bracket is a SyntaxError, and spaces at the start of a line are an IndentationError. Lines that are written correctly fail while running: an unknown name is a NameError, a string joined to a number is a TypeError, and dividing by 0 is a ZeroDivisionError.

=== task: order medium
title: Fix an error, step by step
prompt: Your program stopped with an error. Put these steps in the order you should do them. (Use the arrows to move them.)
items:
- Read the last line of the traceback: the error type and the message.
- Find the line number and go to that line in your code.
- Look at the part of the line that the markers point to.
- Change one thing that matches the usual causes of that error type.
- Run the program again.
- If a new error appears, start again from the top with the new traceback.
hint: A traceback is read from the bottom up. What's at the bottom?
hint: You need to know what kind of problem it is, and on which line, before you change anything.
hint: Fixing comes after reading. After every fix you run again, and a new error starts the whole method again.
right: First understand the error (the last line), then find it (the line number and the markers), then fix one thing, then check by running again. A new error isn't a failure: Python stops at the first error, so each fix can reveal the next one.
wrong: Think of it as read, find, fix, check. Read the last line first, because the error type tells you what to look for. Then go to the line number and the markers. Change one thing, run again, and repeat the whole method if a new error appears.

